{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310869,"nonce":"Hk4WEUpQdEZuDxLqkhHDp/M2goU=","title":"MEASURED census of the 66 `uncompiled` conformance rows — 25 come from ONE root cause (inherited generic unit.Test.eq/aeq never ...","message":"**THE TOOL ALREADY EXISTS: `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`.** It tags every trap stub\nwith its REASON — `[trap-stub:generic]`, `[trap-stub:cascade]`,\n`[skip-stub:fwdref]`, `[safety-net:fwdref-stub]`, plus a `W0020` warning for a\nreal codegen failure. I started writing a MIR-level stub reporter before\nchecking and REVERTED it: it never fired on ANY of the 66, because these stubs\nare installed by the BACKEND (cranelift_backend.rs `define_trap_stub`), not by\nMIR lowering. **Check for an existing diagnostic before building one.**\n\n**CASCADE IS THE KEY.** One real failure stubs every caller:\n`[trap-stub:generic] unit.Test.aeq` -\u003e `[trap-stub:cascade] test calls failed`\n-\u003e `[trap-stub:cascade] main calls failed`. So the runtime message names\n`IssueN.main`, which is THREE levels from the cause. Never diagnose from the\nreported name.\n\n**CENSUS OF ALL 66 (full table: `uncompiled_root_census_2026_09_11.txt`):**\n| n | root |\n|---|---|\n| 25 | `unit.Test.eq\u003cT\u003e` / `aeq\u003cT\u003e` never monomorphized (ONE cause) |\n| 30 | no reason reported — not yet diagnosed |\n| 10 | `W0020` codegen failure, each a different function |\n| 1 | a generic method in the test itself |\n\n**THE 25-ROW LEVER.** `eq\u003cT\u003e(v:T,v2:T)` and `aeq\u003cT\u003e(Array\u003cT\u003e,Array\u003cT\u003e)` live in\nthe shim BASE CLASS `unit.Test`; the test class EXTENDS it. Generic methods\nmonomorphize FINE when declared in the same class — `/tmp/probe/GEN1.hx` does\nall five shapes (literals, locals, `[]` unconstrained, comprehension) and\npasses. So the defect is specific to an INHERITED / cross-module generic.\nRelated: [[project_generic_method_type_args_never_inferred]],\n[[bugs_erased_generic_call_needs_a_bits_coercion]],\n[[bugs_cross_module_inheritance_family]].\n14 of the 25 are `aeq`; the `eq` ones pass an argument whose type is itself\nhard to infer (`Type.getClassFields(C)[0]`, `a.field()`, `Type.enumConstructor`).\n\n**ATTEMPT 2026-09-11 — REVERTED, BUT IT RULED THINGS OUT. READ THIS BEFORE RETRYING.**\n\n1. **The MIR-side fix is IMPOSSIBLE, proven.** `convert_type` maps\n   `TypeKind::Array{element_type}` to `Ptr(Void)` BY DESIGN (\"HaxeArray is an\n   opaque runtime structure\"), so at MIR `aeq`'s signature is\n   `($0:*void,$1:*void,$2:*void)` AND the argument registers are `*void` too.\n   Nothing downstream can recover T. Do not try to extend monomorphize.rs.\n   (`eq\u003cT\u003e(v:T,v2:T)` DOES get `i64` erased slots, which is why its existing\n   erased-slot path works — 9 successes vs 80 skips in one program.)\n2. **The plumbing already exists end to end**: TAST `type_arguments`\n   (node.rs:1157/1165/1189/1268) -\u003e HIR `type_args` -\u003e MIR `type_args` -\u003e\n   monomorphize prints `EXPLICIT type_args=[...]`. The break is ONLY that the\n   typer never fills it: `type_arguments: Vec::new()` at ~12 sites in\n   `ast_lowering/expr/calls.rs`, filled at exactly ONE (`expr/mod.rs:1021`).\n3. **I wired a structural unifier into three of those sites and it NEVER RAN\n   for `aeq`** — instrumented at the bail points, no output at all. So the\n   bare call `aeq(...)` to an INHERITED cross-module method is lowered through\n   NONE of: the two `receiver.method()` MethodCall sites (~1962/1973), the\n   implicit-`this` MethodCall site (~2263), or the fall-through FunctionCall\n   (~2286, instrumented: `make`/`mod`/`div`/`compare` fall through, `aeq` does\n   NOT). **FIND THAT PATH FIRST** — instrument every\n   `TypedExpressionKind::{MethodCall,FunctionCall,StaticMethodCall}`\n   construction at once, or `resolve_method_symbol`, before writing any fix.\n4. Scope any unifier to ONE type parameter: `TypeKind::Function` records no\n   type-param ORDER and MIR consumes `type_args` positionally.\n\n**OPEN TRAP:** a two-file probe (`/tmp/probe/xmod/`) of the same shape fails\nEARLIER, with `Cannot find name 'eq'` — a bare-name inherited PRIVATE method\nacross modules does not even resolve outside the harness's rayzor.toml project\nlayout. Reproduce inside a real project layout (copy a harness case dir from\n`$TMPDIR/rayzor_conformance/run/\u003cIssue\u003e`), not with a lone file.\n\n**THE HARNESS KEEPS EVERYTHING.** `$TMPDIR/rayzor_conformance/logs/\u003cIssue\u003e.log`\nholds every case's full output, and `run/\u003cIssue\u003e/` is a ready-made project dir\nyou can `cd` into and re-run by hand. That is how this census was collected.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_uncompiled_cluster_root_census` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}